function pz_hero_quickanswer_cat_pancreatitis_guide() { ?>
    <div class="pz-hero-quickanswer"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Pancreatitis in cats, inflammation of the pancreas, often causes vague signs like lethargy and reduced appetite rather than the dramatic vomiting seen in dogs, which makes it easy to miss. It's frequently linked to other conditions like inflammatory bowel disease or liver disease, so vets often investigate for all three together. Diagnosis involves bloodwork, a specific pancreatic test (feline pancreatic lipase), and sometimes ultrasound. Treatment is largely supportive: fluids, nutrition support, and anti-nausea care, with most cats improving with prompt veterinary treatment.</div>
