function pz_what_is_cat_pancreatitis_guide() {
    ob_start(); ?>
    <p>Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas, the organ responsible for producing digestive enzymes and regulating blood sugar through insulin. In cats, the exact trigger is often never identified, unlike in dogs where a fatty meal is a classic cause; feline pancreatitis is frequently described as idiopathic, meaning the specific cause isn't determined even after workup.</p>
    <p>According to the Cornell Feline Health Center and veterinary internal medicine sources, cat pancreatitis commonly occurs alongside other conditions, particularly inflammatory bowel disease and cholangitis (liver inflammation), a combination sometimes referred to as "triaditis." This overlap is part of why a cat with vague, chronic symptoms may need a broader workup rather than testing for pancreatitis alone.</p>
    <p>Unlike dogs, cats with pancreatitis often show subtle, nonspecific signs: lethargy, reduced appetite, and mild weight loss are more common than the obvious vomiting and abdominal pain typically seen in canine cases, which makes feline pancreatitis notoriously easy to miss without targeted testing.</p>
    <?php return ob_get_clean();
}

